<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.2.4: Writing Equations to Represent Relationships with Percentages</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00077</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.2.4: Writing Equations to Represent Relationships with Percentages</md:title>
    <md:uuid>3b37bc03-aca6-4573-aad8-edfb7fc009c3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>The tax on the sale of a car in Michigan is 6%. At a dealership in Ann Arbor, a car purchase also involves $120 in miscellaneous charges.</para>
<para>There are several quantities in this situation: the original car price, sales tax, miscellaneous charges, and total price. </para>
<para>For questions 1 - 4, write an equation to describe the relationship between all the quantities when given specific information. </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>The original car price is $9,500.</item>
</list>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\( 𝑇 = 9,500 + 0.06(9,500) + 120 \), or \( 𝑇 = 1.06(9,500) + 120 \)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>The original car price is $14,699.</item>
</list>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\( 𝑇 = 14,699 + 0.06(14,699) + 120 \), or \( 𝑇 = 1.06(14,699) + 120 \)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>The total price is $22,480.</item>
</list>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\( 22,480 = 𝑝 + 0.06𝑝 + 120 \), or \( 22,480 = 1.06𝑝 + 120 \)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>The original price is \( p \).</item>
</list>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\( 𝑇 = 𝑝 + 0.06𝑝 + 120 \), or \( 𝑇 = 1.06𝑝 + 120 \)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> How would each equation you wrote change if the tax on car sales is \( r \)% and the miscellaneous charges are \( m \) dollars?</item>
</list>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
      <item>\( 𝑇 = 9,500 + \frac{r}{100}(9,500)+m \)</item>
      <item>\(𝑇 = 14,699 + \frac{r}{100}(14,699)+m\)</item>
      <item>\(22,480=p+\frac{r}{100}(p)+m\)</item>
      <item>\(T=p+\frac{r}{100}(p)+m\)</item>
    </list>
    <para>Equations equivalent to these are also correct.</para>
  </solution></exercise></section>
